<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="text-center" style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto">
      <span class="section-label">The framing</span>
      <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-inline:auto">A website is an asset that grows over time</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="roi-intro" style="max-width:72ch;margin:1.75rem auto 0">
      <p>Some owners treat a website as an expense to keep small, like office rent. Others expect it to print money, like a slot machine. Neither matches how a small-business website behaves.</p>
      <p>A well-built, well-kept site gains value slowly, and a neglected one loses value slowly. The difference is a phone that rings eight times a week instead of four, added up over years. Month to month, it looks flat. Year to year, you see the curve.</p>
      <p>The rest of this page puts numbers on that idea.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--mist">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="text-center" style="max-width:760px;margin:0 auto">
      <span class="section-label">The four levers</span>
      <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-inline:auto">Four things a well-built site does better</h2>
      <p class="section-subtitle" style="margin-inline:auto">Each is measurable, and each works on its own, but fixing all four is what adds up over a year.</p>
    </div>

    <ol class="roi-levers">
      <li class="roi-lever">
        <span class="roi-lever-num" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
        <h3>Speed keeps visitors who would otherwise leave</h3>
        <p>Google's research has long shown that roughly half of mobile visitors leave a page that takes more than three seconds to load, and more leave with each extra second. A site that scores 55 on Google's speed test loses customers before they ever see it.</p>
        <p class="roi-lever-foot">Moving from a score of 55 to 95 or higher typically wins back 25&ndash;40% of those mobile visitors. For a business with 1,000 monthly visitors from search, 70% on phones, that's 175&ndash;280 more visitors a month who see the site.</p>
      </li>

      <li class="roi-lever">
        <span class="roi-lever-num" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
        <h3>Local search decides whether customers find you on Google</h3>
        <p>For a service business, ranking on Google mostly means appearing in Google's map results (the &ldquo;local pack&rdquo;), the three businesses shown with a map at the top of local searches. They get several times more calls than those just below. Placement depends on matching business details across the web, labels on your site, a complete Google Business Profile, steady new reviews, and thorough service-area pages.</p>
        <p class="roi-lever-foot">With that groundwork in place from launch, a site typically reaches the local pack within 60&ndash;90 days. Without it, a site can stay invisible for years.</p>
      </li>

      <li class="roi-lever">
        <span class="roi-lever-num" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
        <h3>A clear path to contact decides whether visitors call</h3>
        <p>Every page should answer one question: <em>how do I reach this business right now?</em> That means your phone number on the first screen, a form that asks the right questions, tap-to-call and tap-to-text on phones, and a thank-you page that says what happens next.</p>
        <p class="roi-lever-foot">With a clear path, a typical service-business site turns 2&ndash;4% of visitors from search into a call or a form. Without one, it converts a fraction of that.</p>
      </li>

      <li class="roi-lever">
        <span class="roi-lever-num" aria-hidden="true">4</span>
        <h3>Steady upkeep keeps the other three from slipping</h3>
        <p>Every site ages. A plugin-based site with no upkeep collects conflicts, security holes, and broken pages within 12&ndash;18 months. A do-it-yourself builder site won't break that way, but it won't get faster or rank better while better-kept competitors pass it. Because neglect costs nothing you can see in a given month, most small-business sites slowly lose ground.</p>
        <p class="roi-lever-foot">A maintained site keeps its speed, ranking, working forms, and fresh content, with monthly attention from someone who knows what to look for.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section roi-example">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:980px">
    <span class="section-label">The worked example</span>
    <h2 class="section-title" style="max-width:30ch">What the math looks like for a typical local business</h2>

    <p style="max-width:72ch">These figures are a worked example for a typical local business. They show the <em>shape</em> of the math, so plug in your own numbers as you read.</p>

    <h3>Starting assumptions</h3>
    <ul class="roi-stats">
      <li class="roi-stat"><span class="roi-stat-num">1,000</span><span class="roi-stat-label">Monthly visitors from search</span></li>
      <li class="roi-stat"><span class="roi-stat-num">70%</span><span class="roi-stat-label">Share on phones</span></li>
      <li class="roi-stat"><span class="roi-stat-num">$400</span><span class="roi-stat-label">Average job value</span></li>
      <li class="roi-stat"><span class="roi-stat-num">1.2%</span><span class="roi-stat-label">Visitors who get in touch today</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p style="max-width:72ch;color:var(--text-muted);font-size:0.92rem">The example is a residential service business (plumber, electrician, HVAC, roofer, landscaper, or inspector) in a metro of 100,000&ndash;500,000 people, with a do-it-yourself template that scores 55 on Google's speed test, no labels for search engines, and a Google Business Profile that's set up but rarely updated.</p>

    <h3>Before and after, 12 months apart</h3>
    <p style="max-width:72ch">Same traffic sources, same metro, same job value. Different site.</p>

    <div class="roi-ba">
      <article class="roi-ba-card roi-ba--before">
        <span class="roi-ba-eyebrow">Current site &middot; today</span>
        <h4>What the current site loses today</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Speed score <strong>55</strong>, with <strong>35&ndash;45%</strong> of phone visitors leaving before the page loads.</li>
          <li>245&ndash;315 phone visitors a month never see the site.</li>
          <li>About <strong>$1,200&ndash;$1,500 a month</strong> lost to slow loading alone.</li>
          <li>Missing from the local pack, which typically means <strong>30&ndash;50% fewer calls</strong> than a business that appears in it.</li>
          <li>Only <strong>1.2%</strong> of visitors get in touch, because the path from visitor to phone call is unclear.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="roi-ba-card roi-ba--after">
        <span class="roi-ba-eyebrow">Built well &middot; month 12</span>
        <h4>What changes on the same traffic by month 12</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Speed score <strong>95+</strong>, with under <strong>10%</strong> of phone visitors leaving before the page loads.</li>
          <li>A place in the local pack for your main service-and-city searches within <strong>60&ndash;90 days</strong>.</li>
          <li>Visits from search up <strong>40&ndash;80%</strong> as rankings for more specific searches build up.</li>
          <li><strong>2.5&ndash;3.5%</strong> of visitors get in touch as the path to contact gets clearer.</li>
          <li>Combined effect: <strong>2&ndash;4&times; the monthly calls</strong> by month 12.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="roi-bottomline">
      <span class="roi-bottomline-eyebrow">The bottom line</span>
      <p>At a $400 average job and even a modest close rate, the site's yearly cost typically comes to <strong>3&ndash;8% of the extra revenue the site brings in</strong>. In this worked example, that's a return of 12 to 30 times the cost, year after year.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

