      <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-inline:auto">What $175 a month replaces</h2>
      <p class="section-subtitle" style="margin-inline:auto">The plan is $175 a month, with $0 down and a 12-month minimum: $2,100 a year. Whether that's a lot depends on what it replaces.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="roi-cost">
      <article class="roi-cost-card">
        <span class="roi-cost-eyebrow">Compared with doing it yourself</span>
        <h3>The hidden costs of running it yourself</h3>
        <p>A website-builder subscription ($16&ndash;$50 a month), plus unpaid hours in the editor, occasional freelance bills, revenue lost to the gaps above, and hours taken from paying work.</p>
        <p class="roi-cost-tally"><strong>Total:</strong> about the same as the plan, while the site keeps losing customers.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="roi-cost-card">
        <span class="roi-cost-eyebrow">Compared with a monthly retainer</span>
        <h3>The same line item, at a fraction of the cost</h3>
        <p>Most service-business retainers run $500&ndash;$2,500 a month, plus per-change fees and a design deposit. The plan costs roughly a third of the median, with $0 down and no per-change fees.</p>
        <p class="roi-cost-tally"><strong>Total:</strong> 30&ndash;65% less each month.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="roi-cost-card">
        <span class="roi-cost-eyebrow">Compared with a freelancer or part-time hire</span>
        <h3>Upkeep that lasts</h3>
        <p>Freelancers move on or raise their rates, and part-time hires leave. The site slowly declines, and a year later the speed and ranking gap is wider than on day one.</p>
        <p class="roi-cost-tally"><strong>Total:</strong> unpredictable, plus the cost of finding someone new every 12 to 18 months.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p class="roi-cost-foot">Most owners never run this accounting, because the costs are scattered and the lost revenue is invisible. Running it once usually makes the answer clear.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--mist">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:780px">
    <span class="section-label">Where the math has limits</span>
    <h2 class="section-title" style="max-width:30ch">The limits of a good website</h2>

    <p style="max-width:72ch">The math above assumes a few things that aren't always true:</p>

    <ul class="roi-caveats">
      <li><strong>A good website can't fix a business customers don't want to hire.</strong>If the service is overpriced, slow, or poorly regarded locally, the site passes that on. It amplifies the business; it doesn't replace it.</li>
      <li><strong>A good website can't outrank a leader with a 15-year head start.</strong>It can make you competitive where the field is thin or the leaders have let their sites slip. Against strong competitors, it narrows the gap.</li>
      <li><strong>A good website can't replace your offline marketing.</strong>Vehicle wraps, yard signs, referrals, and community work still matter, and the site is the place they all point to.</li>
      <li><strong>A good website can't produce a return in month one.</strong>The example covers 12 months, and most of the growth comes after month three, once Google has spent the first 60 days finding and indexing the site.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="max-width:72ch;margin-top:1.75rem">These limits aren't reasons to skip a good site, only reasons to expect the right things from it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

